# Thornswood, the one door on 443.
#
# This is the reference copy and it matches the live one as of 18 September.
# The live one is C:\Thornswood-Caddy\Caddyfile on the VPS, and until something
# deploys this, the two are kept the same by hand. It lived only on the box
# until now, which meant the only record of how the site was reachable was the
# box itself.
#
# Caddy gets the certificate and renews it on its own. The email below is what
# Let's Encrypt writes to if one is about to lapse.
#
# Keep it in caddy fmt shape. `caddy fmt --overwrite .\Caddyfile` does it, and
# validate complains about the line if you do not.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE VOICE BLOCKS ARE LIVE, AND ON THEIR OWN THEY CHANGE NOTHING.
#
# The voice service still answers on 7778 and 7789 directly as well, so every
# launcher already installed carries on exactly as before. These blocks add a
# second way in, through the certificate, for clients that know to use it.
#
# handle_path strips the prefix, so /voice/health reaches the service as
# /health and everything it serves keeps working unchanged. Caddy carries a
# WebSocket upgrade through reverse_proxy without being told to, so the
# signalling socket needs nothing more than this.
#
# What is NOT done yet, in this order:
#   1. the clients still build ws://host:port and must learn to use wss
#   2. those clients have to ship and go out
#   3. only then THORNSWOOD_VOICE_BIND=127.0.0.1 and the two ports close
#
# Doing 3 before 2 takes voice from everybody, and no change here brings them
# back, because the raw address is compiled into the copy on their machine.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

{
	email datdude365d@gmail.com
}

thornswood.net, www.thornswood.net {
	encode gzip

	handle_path /voice/* {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:7778
	}

	handle_path /voice-dev/* {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:7789
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3001
}
